1546		A directly indexed sub-line law $D_{\mathrm{dir}}$ is a probability law
1547		on triples $(\ell,\ell_X,\ell_Z)$ with the following properties.
1548		Its extended-line marginal is the line marginal just defined.
1549		Every supported triple admits affine maps $t\mapsto a_W+b_Wt$ such that
1550		\[
1551			\pi_W(\ell(t))=\ell_W(a_W+b_Wt),\qquad W\in\{X,Z\},
1552		\]
1553		where $\ell(t)$ denotes base plus parameter times direction and the
1554		field identifications are understood. Thus each extended point projects
1555		onto its two source lines, with affine scalar coordinates $\alpha(t)$
1556		and $\beta(t)$ as in Definition~\ref{def:combine-map}. An axis-parallel
1557		extended line has axis-parallel source lines.
1558	
1559		Finally there is one probability law on
1560		$(\mathrm v,i,j)\in\{\mathrm{ALine},\mathrm{DLine}\}
1561		\times\{0,\dots,m-1\}^2$ with the following two mixture properties.
1562		Sample a triple from $D_{\mathrm{dir}}$ and an independent uniform
1563		parameter $t$ on $\ell$. The law of $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,\pi_X(\ell(t)))$
1564		is the mixture obtained by sampling $(\ell_X,x)$ from
1565		$D_{\mathrm v,i}$ and independently $\ell_Z$ from the line marginal of
1566		$D_{\mathrm v,j}$, and retaining $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,x)$.
1567		The law of $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,\pi_Z(\ell(t)))$ is the mixture obtained
1568		by retaining $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,z)$ from the corresponding independent
1569		samples of $\ell_X$ and $(\ell_Z,z)$. These are separate one-point
1570		marginal identities, with the same mixture weights; they impose no
1571		joint conditional independence of the two projected points.
1572		Identification with the source law $D$ is a separate obligation;
1573		see \cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.
1574	\end{definition}
